[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known. It is two bugs. Both flip a
date, but they have different causes and different fixes. One fix does not close the other.

## What the table shows

Intake tags each report from the reporter's browser. That tag is a guess. The cause comes from
the tenant: the locale it writes dates in, and the booking form it runs. The table below groups
every report merged into #4471 by that cause. It counts each report id once.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The row marked as not this ticket is the second bug. It has no ticket of its own in the desk.
We filed one ticket per bug:

- **parser**: the shared parser reads every date month first, so day-first tenants get flipped
  dates. Issue: [what-it-shows#1](https://github.com/andresantosnl/what-it-shows/issues/1).
- **hint**: booking-v3 prints a month-first hint under the date field. Day-first tenants type
  what the hint says and the form reads it day first. The fix is the hint or the form, not the
  parser. Issue: [what-it-shows#2](https://github.com/andresantosnl/what-it-shows/issues/2).

<Callout kind="warn">
Intake tags do not tell the causes apart. Some parser reports are tagged hint, and some hint
reports are tagged parser. Count by tenant, not by tag.
</Callout>

## Why they were merged

The desk merges on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
Both bugs produce the same subjects, such as "Wrong day on the invite". A subject cannot tell a
parser report from a hint report, so the merge hid the second bug behind the first.

## Open question

The desk read a few reports as a one-day move, not a month flip. They mention time zones. They
stay counted under their tenant's cause above. They may be a third bug, a time-zone one. Read
them before closing either ticket.

## Lessons

- Merging on subject counts the same words, not the same bug. Merge on cause.
- A number in a page comes from a component. This page types none.

